-- dnip: promoted from tools/spec_r528.json (the The DSG IMPROVING PLAY OPENER: EXPEDITION PLANNING, BEASTS OF BURDEN, WATERCRAFT, R528: printed pp. 59-60 (pdf 60-61) - the frame (game habits causing problems, the recommendations grouped into expedition planning, speeding play, and effective fighting) and the expedition planning: the success or failure often determined before leaving base - more often the failure - so learn everything possible first (NPC interviews, the approaches and periphery, magic for insight, even rumors having a basis in fact, the basilisk slithering meaning mirrors); scout with light avoid-combat parties (one or two thieves, invisibility spells, gaseous-form potions) and keep scouting during the adventure (the listening-at-doors function, one character looking before the group enters, cutting ambush risk and raising tactical options); and the type-of-expedition preparation - objectives, duration and areas considered, ready to climb or descend cliffs, cross water, provide light, feed for an extended period and still return, extra food for opportunity, foot travel on the rough going, waterways the easy fast access for boaters but notoriously dangerous and unpredictable (the placid stream turning cascade or vanishing crack), beasts of burden where smooth; the mules: 2,000 gp of evenly distributed saddlebag weight no encumbrance, 6,000 gp maximum at 1/2 speed refusing over eight hours a day, the 50% persuadable six-foot-or-less chasm (never wider, never loaded over 2,000, no mind-changing on failure), average-steepness stairs and human footing with hands-required slopes too steep, the 50% very-steep slope, the 90% willing swim and six-foot jump into water (refusing over 2,000 gp), the 3-inch-per-round swim, the string following its leader (a refused leader unhitched, never changing its mind), the strange-odor jitters at 25% bolt/bray/freeze/normal (one check for the group), and the 360-ft stone noise muffled to 90 ft with leather or cloth boots replaced daily; the dogs: the 100-lb war-dog varieties (shepherds, malamutes, wolfhounds), trained per the animal-trainer proficiency, 200 gp without penalty, 500 gp maximum at half speed and -4 to all attacks, no swimming or steep climbing over 200 gp, the 50% attack-strangers tendency (restrained dogs barking instead), the advanced-training discipline (no attack or bark unless directed, hand signals needing sight), and the keen-smell-and-hearing darkness penalties of -2 instead of -4; and the watercraft: the kayak nimblest and roughest-capable but requiring small-boat proficiency from all characters, larger craft carrying more together but limited in rough or narrow waters, the collapsible boat flexible but bulky and less sturdy, waterborne scouting the more vital (the waterway ascertained ahead, the one-way current needing a return plan));
-- DO NOT hand-edit; the spec JSON stays the adjudicated record.
return {
  "round" = 528,
  "prefix" = "dnip",
  "banner" = {
      "title" = "The DSG IMPROVING PLAY OPENER: EXPEDITION PLANNING, BEASTS OF BURDEN, WATERCRAFT, R528: printed pp. 59-60 (pdf 60-61) - the frame (game habits causing problems, the recommendations grouped into expedition planning, speeding play, and effective fighting) and the expedition planning: the success or failure often determined before leaving base - more often the failure - so learn everything possible first (NPC interviews, the approaches and periphery, magic for insight, even rumors having a basis in fact, the basilisk slithering meaning mirrors); scout with light avoid-combat parties (one or two thieves, invisibility spells, gaseous-form potions) and keep scouting during the adventure (the listening-at-doors function, one character looking before the group enters, cutting ambush risk and raising tactical options); and the type-of-expedition preparation - objectives, duration and areas considered, ready to climb or descend cliffs, cross water, provide light, feed for an extended period and still return, extra food for opportunity, foot travel on the rough going, waterways the easy fast access for boaters but notoriously dangerous and unpredictable (the placid stream turning cascade or vanishing crack), beasts of burden where smooth; the mules: 2,000 gp of evenly distributed saddlebag weight no encumbrance, 6,000 gp maximum at 1/2 speed refusing over eight hours a day, the 50% persuadable six-foot-or-less chasm (never wider, never loaded over 2,000, no mind-changing on failure), average-steepness stairs and human footing with hands-required slopes too steep, the 50% very-steep slope, the 90% willing swim and six-foot jump into water (refusing over 2,000 gp), the 3-inch-per-round swim, the string following its leader (a refused leader unhitched, never changing its mind), the strange-odor jitters at 25% bolt/bray/freeze/normal (one check for the group), and the 360-ft stone noise muffled to 90 ft with leather or cloth boots replaced daily; the dogs: the 100-lb war-dog varieties (shepherds, malamutes, wolfhounds), trained per the animal-trainer proficiency, 200 gp without penalty, 500 gp maximum at half speed and -4 to all attacks, no swimming or steep climbing over 200 gp, the 50% attack-strangers tendency (restrained dogs barking instead), the advanced-training discipline (no attack or bark unless directed, hand signals needing sight), and the keen-smell-and-hearing darkness penalties of -2 instead of -4; and the watercraft: the kayak nimblest and roughest-capable but requiring small-boat proficiency from all characters, larger craft carrying more together but limited in rough or narrow waters, the collapsible boat flexible but bulky and less sturdy, waterborne scouting the more vital (the waterway ascertained ahead, the one-way current needing a return plan)",
      "provenance" = "the DSG scan (archive.org item advanced-dungeons-dragons-1st-edition), pdf = printed + 1; the text read from the IA djvu OCR (djvu.txt lines ~7564-7848) with the pdf Acrobat-Capture layer as the second read (idx 137 = the printed p. 59 window, idx 139 = the printed p. 60 window); the compiled-tables appendix has no reprint of this prose. A CLEAN-READ ROUND: both machine reads agree on every load-bearing number - the mule 2,000/6,000 gp with the half-speed and 8-hour rules, the 50% chasm at six feet, the 50% very-steep slope, the 90% swim, the 3-inch swim rate, the 25% odor quartet, the 360/90-ft noise, the dog 200/500 gp with the -4, the 100-pound weight, the 50% stranger attack, and the -2-not--4 darkness. The only garbles are the standing glyph classes (djvu \"5096\" reading as 50%, the \"\u00fcsing\" lead). SEAMS: the Monster Manual mule and war dog definitions (cross-book), the animal-trainer proficiency (R506/R527), the small-boat proficiency (R518 water travel), and the R526 collapsible boat. The improving-play chapter split per the content-density rule: this round covers children 142-144 (the planning opener), and R529 takes fighting effectively, diversions, speeding play, creative spell use, languages and mapping (children 145-150). Nothing auto-credits.",
      "sentinel" = "A prose round: no tables pin. The numbers pin as consts per the R508 convention: the mule weights (2,000 free, 6,000 max), the half-speed divisor and 8-hour cap, the 50% chasm at 6 ft and the 50% steep slope, the 90% water willingness with the 6-ft jump, the 3-inch swim, the 25% odor quartet, the 360/90-ft noise, the dog weights (200/500 gp, 100 lbs), the -4 overload penalty, the 50% stranger attack, and the -2 darkness penalty. The rest pins as prose bools: the three-category frame, the early-determined success, the scouting and rumor rules, the expedition preparation, the transport tradeoffs, the mule string/footing/stairs rules, the dog training and discipline, and the watercraft comparisons. The Monster Manual, R506/R527, and R518/R526 seams are noted; nothing auto-credits.",
    },
  "consts" = { {
      "name" = "dnipMuleFreeWtGp",
      "value" = 2000,
      "comment" = "up to 2,000 gp of weight, evenly distributed in saddlebags, does not encumber a mule.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleMaxWtGp",
      "value" = 6000,
      "comment" = "a mule carries up to a maximum of 6,000 gp at half speed.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleOverHalfSpeedDiv",
      "value" = 2,
      "comment" = "a mule loaded over 2,000 gp travels at 1/2 speed.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleMaxWalkHours",
      "value" = 8,
      "comment" = "an overloaded mule refuses to walk for more than eight hours a day.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleChasmPct",
      "value" = 50,
      "comment" = "there is a 50% chance that a mule can be persuaded to jump a chasm six feet wide or less - and it never jumps a wider gap, and never while carrying more than 2,000 gp.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleChasmMaxFt",
      "value" = 6,
      "comment" = "the persuadable chasm is six feet wide or less.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleSteepPct",
      "value" = 50,
      "comment" = "there is a 50% chance that a mule can be persuaded to climb or descend a very steep slope.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleWaterPct",
      "value" = 90,
      "comment" = "there is a 90% probability that a mule is willing to swim or jump into water - though it refuses to enter water carrying more than 2,000 gp.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleJumpHeightFt",
      "value" = 6,
      "comment" = "a mule jumps from a height of up to six feet to enter water.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleSwimInches",
      "value" = 3,
      "comment" = "a swimming mule travels at a rate of 3 inches (30 feet) per round.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleOdorPct",
      "value" = 25,
      "comment" = "an odor-alarmed mule bolts (25%), brays in panic (25%), freezes in place (25%), or functions normally (25%) - a single check for a whole group of mules.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleNoiseFt",
      "value" = 360,
      "comment" = "mules walking on stone make noise audible to a distance of 360 feet.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleMuffledNoiseFt",
      "value" = 90,
      "comment" = "leather or cloth boots wrapped around the hooves reduce the audible distance to 90 feet - the muffles must be replaced daily.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogFreeWtGp",
      "value" = 200,
      "comment" = "a trained dog fitted with small saddlebags carries up to 200 gp without penalties to movement or combat.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogMaxWtGp",
      "value" = 500,
      "comment" = "a dog can be loaded with up to 500 gp of weight.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogOverloadAtkPenalty",
      "value" = 4,
      "comment" = "an overloaded dog moves at half speed and suffers a -4 modifier to all attacks - and cannot swim or climb steep slopes over 200 gp.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogWeightLbs",
      "value" = 100,
      "comment" = "the war-dog varieties described generally weigh about 100 pounds.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogStrangerPct",
      "value" = 50,
      "comment" = "dogs have a 50% chance to attack anything that does not belong to their party unless physically restrained - restrained dogs that would attack instead bark loudly and savagely.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogDarkPenalty",
      "value" = 2,
      "comment" = "a dog's penalties for attacking, damage, and AC in complete darkness are all -2 instead of the normal -4.",
    } },
  "bools" = { {
      "name" = "dnipThreeCategories",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the improving-play tactics are grouped into three categories: expedition planning, speeding play of the game, and effective fighting.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipSuccessDeterminedEarly",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the success or failure of an expedition is often determined before the party leaves base - more often the failure, from a neglected vital feature, an essential piece of equipment, or missing information.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipLearnEverythingFirst",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "before leaving, characters should make strong efforts to learn everything possible about the setting: NPC interviews, exploring the approaches and periphery, and using magic for insight.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipRumorsHaveBasis",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "even rumors should not be disregarded, for such talk often has a basis in fact - basilisk rumors mean wise characters bring mirrors.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipScoutLightAvoidCombat",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a scouting mission comprises characters traveling much more lightly than normal, avoiding combat at all costs - one or two thieves, possibly aided by invisibility spells, potions of gaseous form, or other magical protection.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipScoutDuringAdventure",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "scouting continues during the adventure: listening at a door before smashing it is a scouting function, and at least one character should look at an area before the whole group enters - reducing ambush risk and increasing tactical options.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipExpeditionPlanningNeeds",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "an underground expedition of any length needs considerable planning: the objectives, the length of time, and the areas to explore all considered, with the objective ideally a task motivating the PCs.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipPrepareForTerrain",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the PCs must be prepared to climb or descend cliffs, cross water by swimming or boat, provide light sources, feed themselves for an extended period, and still return to the surface.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipExtraFoodCarried",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "smart adventurers carry more food than needed, so opportunities for further adventuring are not restricted by supply considerations.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipRoughGoingOnFoot",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if the going is extremely rough - steep descents and narrow passages - the characters must travel on foot, carrying everything in backpacks.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipWaterwaysEasyAccess",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "waterways often provide easy access to underground regions: boating characters carry more equipment and travel faster than walkers.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipWaterwaysDangerous",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "underground waterways are notoriously dangerous and unpredictable - a placid stream can become a churning cascade or disappear through a crack, blocking exploration.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleMostCommon",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "mules are the most commonly employed beasts of burden - somewhat reliable supply mounts that walk as far as any character.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleStairsAndFooting",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a mule walks and climbs stairways of average steepness or a little steeper, keeping its footing as well as a human - but a slope requiring hands is too steep.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleStringLeader",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "mules in a string all follow the leader: if the leader performs a task the others automatically follow, and a refusing leader can be unhitched for the next mule to be checked - the old leader never changes its mind.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipMuleOdorJittery",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "mules tend to be very jittery at strange smells, and a party employing mules can always be followed.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogWarDogVarieties",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the dogs described are all varieties of the war dog (per the Monster Manual), resembling large German shepherds (Alsatians), malamutes, or wolfhounds.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogTrainingRequired",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a dog must have animal-trainer proficiency training before serving as a pack animal, and can negotiate slopes of the same steepness as a mule.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogAdvancedDiscipline",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a dog with successfully completed advanced training is much more disciplined - not attacking or barking unless directed by its master, and trainable to hand signals it must see to obey.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipDogKeenSenses",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "dogs have no infravision, but their keen senses of smell and hearing aid them in darkness.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipKayakMostNimble",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the kayak is certainly the most nimble craft, capable of handling the roughest water - but all characters must have small-boat proficiency for a kayak expedition to succeed.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipLargerCraftTradeoff",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "larger craft have greater cargo capacity and keep the PCs together in one boat, but much more limited utility in rough or narrow waters.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipCollapsibleTradeoff",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "collapsible boats allow flexible planning, carried overland for stretches, but are bulky and difficult to carry for long distances and not as sturdy as other vessels.",
    }, {
      "name" = "dnipWaterScoutVital",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if a waterborne expedition is planned, scouting becomes even more important: the nature of the waterway must be ascertained as far ahead as possible, and if a current is relied upon one way, some other means of return must be arranged.",
    } },
  "switches" = {},
  "tables" = {},
  "chains" = {},
  "examples" = { {
      "expr" = "m['dnipMuleFreeWtGp']() != 2000 or m['dnipMuleMaxWtGp']() != 6000 or m['dnipMuleOverHalfSpeedDiv']() != 2 or m['dnipMuleMaxWalkHours']() != 8 or m['dnipMuleChasmPct']() != 50 or m['dnipMuleChasmMaxFt']() != 6",
      "note" = "the mule load numbers: 2,000 gp free, 6,000 gp max at half speed and eight hours, and the 50% six-foot chasm.",
    }, {
      "expr" = "m['dnipMuleSteepPct']() != 50 or m['dnipMuleWaterPct']() != 90 or m['dnipMuleJumpHeightFt']() != 6 or m['dnipMuleSwimInches']() != 3 or m['dnipMuleOdorPct']() != 25 or m['dnipMuleNoiseFt']() != 360 or m['dnipMuleMuffledNoiseFt']() != 90",
      "note" = "the mule action numbers: the 50% steep slope, the 90% swim with the six-foot jump, the 3-inch swim, the 25% odor reactions, and the 360/90-ft noise.",
    }, {
      "expr" = "m['dnipDogFreeWtGp']() != 200 or m['dnipDogMaxWtGp']() != 500 or m['dnipDogOverloadAtkPenalty']() != 4 or m['dnipDogWeightLbs']() != 100 or m['dnipDogStrangerPct']() != 50 or m['dnipDogDarkPenalty']() != 2",
      "note" = "the dog numbers: 200 gp free and 500 gp max at -4, the 100-pound war dog, the 50% stranger attack, and the -2 darkness penalties.",
    }, {
      "expr" = "m['dnipThreeCategories']() != 1 or m['dnipRumorsHaveBasis']() != 1 or m['dnipScoutLightAvoidCombat']() != 1 or m['dnipExtraFoodCarried']() != 1 or m['dnipMuleStringLeader']() != 1 or m['dnipKayakMostNimble']() != 1 or m['dnipWaterScoutVital']() != 1",
      "note" = "the planning prose: the three categories, the rumor basis, the light scouting, the extra food, the mule-string leader rule, the kayak proficiency requirement, and the waterborne return-means rule.",
    } },
  "audit" = {
      "label" = "R528 DSG improving-play opener audit",
      "comment" = "The DSG improving-play opener pins the expedition planning (the three categories, the early-determined success, the scouting and rumor rules, the preparation list, and the transport tradeoffs), the beasts of burden (the mule 2,000/6,000 gp loads, the 50% chasm and steep-slope persuasions, the 90% swim at 3 inches per round, the string-leader rule, the 25% odor quartet, the 360/90-ft noise; and the dog 200/500 gp loads at -4, the 50% stranger attack, the advanced-training discipline, and the -2 darkness penalties), and the watercraft (the kayak proficiency requirement, the larger-craft and collapsible tradeoffs, and the waterborne scouting imperative).",
    },
}

-- dnpm: promoted from tools/spec_r529.json (the The DSG IMPROVING-PLAY TAIL: FIGHTING, DIVERSIONS, SPEEDING PLAY, SPELL USE, LANGUAGES, MAPPING, R529: printed pp. 60-63 (pdf 61-64) - the fighting effectively: characters fight better together long (experience and trust, but cooperative techniques practicable from the start), the well-organized party defining roles (the low-AC fighters between the monsters and the rest), the retreat plans (the rear guard, the pursuit-discouraging magic-user spell, the safe-path scout), the mini-teams (the fighter bodyguarding the mage who casts for the group; the diversion fighter drawing attention while the thief sneaks behind), and surprise probably the single most important tactic (scouting to avoid it, silent no-light movement to cause it; rangers least susceptible and most likely to cause it, human rangers grievously disadvantaged underground by their lack of infravision, half-elven rangers very effective); the creating diversions: a distraction can substitute for concealment (fires demanding immediate attention, collapsing bridges/tunnels/dams, the rope-topped statue), monsters not automatically falling for it - the low-Intelligence reasoning monsters like orcs and ogres distracted 1d4 rounds, the medium-to-high Intelligence allowed a saving throw vs. spell, the genius-level fooled only by very clever diversions concealing the PCs' presence, and player characters never saving against monster diversions (purely role playing); the speeding play: pace as group taste but wasted time a bane to all (squabbling, non-cooperation, DM-arguing, indecision), fixed by standard procedures - the door-opening drill (a sketch with each character's role, declared as "door opening drill #2"), the standard marching orders (corridor widths, open areas, weapons carried, changes communicated), the caller re-examined for frustrated groups (delegating to one member, rotating every session), and the bossy player best handled by peer pressure; the creative underground spell use: the balanced selection across offensive, defensive, restorative, investigatigative and scouting spells with reverses considered, the sleep protections (magic mouth, glyphs of warding, fire traps, rope tricks), the underground-deadly cloudkill/stinking cloud/dig/transmute rock to mud/disintegrate with augury/divination/find the path useful, the raise-water flood from high terrain and lower water against enemy boaters, the enlarge/reduce/warp wood passage tricks, the silence coin carried for the party and thrown among enemy spellcasters, the darkness against infravision creatures (dramatic with blindfighting proficiency), the telekinesis-positioned slime pots dropped as missiles or left in the path and broken with shatter, the charm-aided questioning of captives, and the toppling walls of stone/ice/iron with the ricocheting lightning bolt; the languages of the underdark: the UA undercommon and sign language plus the tapping language of the subterranean races (clicking stones, drum beats, sharp-sound patterns), the underground reverberations amplifying drum communication over very long distances, the roughly 10-times-slower pace, and the underground-native PCs knowing it; and the mapping techniques: the detailed 10-foot-block graphing standard (accurate but slow in game and real time, the mapper's light source exposing the party, the 10-to-20-foot map errors agonized over against teleport-trap worries), the exit-purpose rule making elaborate maps overkill, and the line-drawing alternative (lines for corridors, door symbols, guesswork distances, the explored-versus-unexplored display, the 100-foot sketch with temporary light, sufficient everywhere except mazes and convoluted cavern networks));
-- DO NOT hand-edit; the spec JSON stays the adjudicated record.
return {
  "round" = 529,
  "prefix" = "dnpm",
  "banner" = {
      "title" = "The DSG IMPROVING-PLAY TAIL: FIGHTING, DIVERSIONS, SPEEDING PLAY, SPELL USE, LANGUAGES, MAPPING, R529: printed pp. 60-63 (pdf 61-64) - the fighting effectively: characters fight better together long (experience and trust, but cooperative techniques practicable from the start), the well-organized party defining roles (the low-AC fighters between the monsters and the rest), the retreat plans (the rear guard, the pursuit-discouraging magic-user spell, the safe-path scout), the mini-teams (the fighter bodyguarding the mage who casts for the group; the diversion fighter drawing attention while the thief sneaks behind), and surprise probably the single most important tactic (scouting to avoid it, silent no-light movement to cause it; rangers least susceptible and most likely to cause it, human rangers grievously disadvantaged underground by their lack of infravision, half-elven rangers very effective); the creating diversions: a distraction can substitute for concealment (fires demanding immediate attention, collapsing bridges/tunnels/dams, the rope-topped statue), monsters not automatically falling for it - the low-Intelligence reasoning monsters like orcs and ogres distracted 1d4 rounds, the medium-to-high Intelligence allowed a saving throw vs. spell, the genius-level fooled only by very clever diversions concealing the PCs' presence, and player characters never saving against monster diversions (purely role playing); the speeding play: pace as group taste but wasted time a bane to all (squabbling, non-cooperation, DM-arguing, indecision), fixed by standard procedures - the door-opening drill (a sketch with each character's role, declared as \"door opening drill #2\"), the standard marching orders (corridor widths, open areas, weapons carried, changes communicated), the caller re-examined for frustrated groups (delegating to one member, rotating every session), and the bossy player best handled by peer pressure; the creative underground spell use: the balanced selection across offensive, defensive, restorative, investigatigative and scouting spells with reverses considered, the sleep protections (magic mouth, glyphs of warding, fire traps, rope tricks), the underground-deadly cloudkill/stinking cloud/dig/transmute rock to mud/disintegrate with augury/divination/find the path useful, the raise-water flood from high terrain and lower water against enemy boaters, the enlarge/reduce/warp wood passage tricks, the silence coin carried for the party and thrown among enemy spellcasters, the darkness against infravision creatures (dramatic with blindfighting proficiency), the telekinesis-positioned slime pots dropped as missiles or left in the path and broken with shatter, the charm-aided questioning of captives, and the toppling walls of stone/ice/iron with the ricocheting lightning bolt; the languages of the underdark: the UA undercommon and sign language plus the tapping language of the subterranean races (clicking stones, drum beats, sharp-sound patterns), the underground reverberations amplifying drum communication over very long distances, the roughly 10-times-slower pace, and the underground-native PCs knowing it; and the mapping techniques: the detailed 10-foot-block graphing standard (accurate but slow in game and real time, the mapper's light source exposing the party, the 10-to-20-foot map errors agonized over against teleport-trap worries), the exit-purpose rule making elaborate maps overkill, and the line-drawing alternative (lines for corridors, door symbols, guesswork distances, the explored-versus-unexplored display, the 100-foot sketch with temporary light, sufficient everywhere except mazes and convoluted cavern networks)",
      "provenance" = "the DSG scan (archive.org item advanced-dungeons-dragons-1st-edition), pdf = printed + 1; the text read from the IA djvu OCR (djvu.txt lines ~7848-8160) with the pdf Acrobat-Capture layer as the second read (idx 139-143 = the printed pp. 60-63 windows); the compiled-tables appendix has no reprint of this prose. A CLEAN-READ ROUND: both machine reads agree on every load-bearing number - the 1d4-round diversion distraction, the 10x tapping-language slowness, the 10-ft graphing blocks, the 10-or-20-ft map error, and the 100-ft sketch window. The only garbles are the standing glyph classes (the UA-lead djvu noise, the \"5096\"-class absent here). SEAMS: the Unearthed Arcana undercommon and sign language (cross-book), the blindfighting proficiency (R506), the ranger infravision (PHB class), the R518 water travel, and the R511 surprise rules. WITH THIS ROUND THE IMPROVING-PLAY CHAPTER CLOSES (all children 142-150 flipped, the parent 141 closeable at the next census check) - and the player half of the book through printed p. 63 is fully pinned. The DM's section (pp. 64-120, re-grained at R503) starts the NEXT round. Nothing auto-credits.",
      "sentinel" = "A prose round: no tables pin. The numbers pin as consts per the R508 convention: the 1d4-round diversion distraction, the 10x tapping-language multiplier, and the mapping numbers (the 10-ft blocks, the 10/20-ft error pair, the 100-ft sketch). The rest pins as prose bools: the fighting rules (the togetherness, the low-AC placement, the retreat plans, the mini-teams, the surprise paramountcy with the ranger note), the diversion tiers (the fire/sabotage/remote-control set, the low-Int 1d4, the mid/high save, the genius standard, the PC no-save), the speeding-play procedures (the drills, the marching orders, the caller rotation, the peer pressure), the creative spell tactics (the balanced selection, the sleep protections, the deadly set, the water spells, the passage tricks, the silence coin, the darkness, the slime pots, the charm, the toppling walls), the tapping language (the drum amplification, the native-PC knowledge), and the mapping styles (the graphing weaknesses, the exit purpose, the line-drawing alternative and its limits). The UA/R506/R511/R518 seams are noted; nothing auto-credits.",
    },
  "consts" = { {
      "name" = "dnpmDivertDice",
      "value" = 1,
